# The Phase 1 fidelity subject: run `macchina`, a real system-info TUI that
# exercises coloured labels, a vertical bar, Unicode glyphs and a busy grid of
# short text runs — the kind of output a recording tool is actually judged on,
# as opposed to a bare shell prompt. `scripts/inspect/compare.sh` burns this
# tape and burns `macchina.vhs.tape` (the closest VHS can get to the same
# terminal) and diffs the two.
#
# Every `Set` below has a value chosen to match the VHS twin as closely as the
# two languages allow. Anything without a `dvd` equivalent is noted there, not
# here, since this is the file that has everything `dvd` supports.

Set Shell "bash"
Set FontFamily "Menlo"
Set FontSize 18
Set Width 1200
Set Height 700
Set Padding 24
Set Theme "dracula"
Set Framerate 30
Set TypingSpeed 40ms
# `true` here purely to stay textually identical to the VHS twin (see that
# file's own comment on this line) — not to dodge a bug. `Set CursorBlink
# false` used to make `burn.rs` write the raw byte sequence `\x1b[?12l`
# through `session.write`, delivering it to the PTY as if a user had typed
# it; the shell's line editor could not interpret a DECRST-12 control
# sequence arriving as keyboard input and spliced fragments of it into the
# next keystrokes (the W7 report). `session::Session::feed` now applies that
# escape straight to the VT parser instead — the same path real terminal
# *output* takes — so the shell never sees it and `false` is safe again.
Set CursorBlink true

# No longer load-bearing: `direct()` now blocks on the shell's own prompt
# actually appearing (the same pattern `Wait` matches) before the first
# `Type`, so nothing can race the boot banner. Left in as extra margin for
# `macchina`'s own startup rather than the shell's, and because removing it
# buys nothing.
Sleep 500ms

Type "macchina"
Enter

# `Wait` blocks on the shell prompt reappearing, i.e. until macchina has
# finished printing and control has returned — the same signal a human would
# wait for before taking a screenshot.
Wait

# macchina's own output settles the instant it prints, but real terminals and
# `vhs`'s ttyd+chromium capture path both need a beat to stop redrawing; this
# keeps the still comparable to VHS's without becoming the thing being timed.
Sleep 2s
